  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 AM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nAn elongated mid 990s surface low continues to shift towards the\nHudson/James Bays and is dragging an associated cold front \nacross the Upper Midwest early this morning. This front will\npush across our forecast through midday/early afternoon and will\nflip winds out of the northwest as it passes. Efficient mixing\nin the wake of the boundary will facilitate northwesterly\nbreezes and perhaps some sporadic 20 mph gusts. While there\nremains considerable spread in the handling of afternoon\ndewpoints, have sided with the deeper-mixing guidance based on\nupstream observations and the well-mixed nature of forecast\nsoundings today. This has brought afternoon RH values generally\ndown to around 35 percent away from the influence of the lake.\nAir temperatures will rise into the mid-upper 70s north of I-80\nand locally into the lower 80s to the south. \n\nHigh pressure will slide overhead on Thursday, and highs will\nend up about 4 to 8 degrees cooler than today. On Friday,\nincreasing southwesterly flow will drive an increase in low-\nlevel warm advection across the Great Lakes and Upper Midwest.\nAscent at the northeastern terminus of a steepened mid-level\nlapse rate plume still looks like it has the potential to result\nin at least a low chance for some showers in the region Friday \nmorning and afternoon. It's still a bit unclear how much mid- \nlevel saturation will take place before the main zone of WAA \nexits northward, but some broad-brushed slight chance PoPs seem\nappropriate near and west of the Fox Valley for now. Latest \nruns of the ECMWF remain decidedly more bullish regarding precip \ncoverage and intensity across northern Illinois into Friday \nafternoon, even including a threat for some lightning. This is \nstill somewhat of an outlier solution at this point, but will \ncontinue to keep an eye on this period given the instability \npresent aloft. \n\nThe day on Saturday looks dry, but high-level moisture in the \nform of increasing cirrus will stream northward across the area \nthrough the afternoon. Depending on how thick/opaque this is, \ncould envision this impacting high temperatures a bit, although \nfairly stout southerly flow will still deliver unseasonable warm\nconditions to the forecast area.  \n\nTrends in global guidance over the last 24 hours have been\ntowards a modestly farther west track of a broad trough\ninteracting with a northward-advancing tropical system (TD 9\npresently) in the northern Gulf. Ensemble and deterministic\nguidance still keeps the main upper wave off to our south and\neast, but close enough to toss some rain chances across parts of\nthe CWA Saturday night into Sunday. The ECMWF and its ensembles\nhave remained fairly consistent with a farther west solution\ninto Sunday, and NBM-delivered PoPs have commensurately come up\na bit for areas east of about I-55. \n\nThe latest ECMWF perhaps has a modestly enhanced threat for a \nlocalized heavy rain threat (even though it is not producing a \nhigh amount of QPF) into central Indiana. This is where the \noverlap of tropical system-assisted upper divergence within the\nright entrance region of a modest 90 kt jet may interact with \nthe northern fringes of an advancing pool of deep moisture along\na low-level theta ridge. At this time, there seems to be a bit \nof a mismatch between the salient parameters (namely the deepest\nmoisture/highest PWAT airmass lagging the main zone of forcing),\nand not noting anything overly concerning at this point from a \nPredecessor Rainfall Event (PRE) standpoint. \n\nAnother cold front is slated to arrive in the region early next\nweek with another potential for showers and associated brief cool-\ndown. \n\nCarlaw",
